# The palette. Single source of truth for every themed surface on this machine.
#
# It is not a taste decision and it is not copied from anyone: it is the `cool`
# colormap — the same one matplotlib/matplotlibrc makes the default for every
# plot this machine draws — sampled evenly and pastelised toward white, exactly
# as cool_pastel() does in matplotlib:
#
#     cool(t)  = (255t, 255(1-t), 255)          cyan -> magenta
#     pastel   = c + (255 - c) * strength
#
# ONE palette, and that is the decision rather than an unfinished job.
#
# The coupling above is the whole point: because the desktop and the figures are
# the same ramp, btop's temperature gradient and a hot line in a plot are the
# same colour. A second SHIPPED palette would break exactly that -- a "warm
# mode" puts the desktop and the figures in different languages, and then the
# palette is only a look.
#
# That argument is about someone who plots in cool. Anyone who does not inherits
# none of it, so the palette is a parameter:
#
#     ergon theme ~/mine.env        ERGON_PALETTE=~/mine.env ergon theme
#
# The default stays the one that is tested and coherent.
#
# strength 0.68 is the documented look and reproduces glow/cool.json byte for
# byte (#ADFFFF / #D6D6FF / #FFADFF are t = 0, 0.5, 1). A second ramp at 0.25 is
# kept for surfaces that must read as an alert rather than as decoration.
#
# Nothing reads this file at runtime. bin/ergon-theme expands it into the
# generated configs beside each .in template -- into the repo, not into $HOME,
# because install.sh links the repo into $HOME and a file written straight to
# the destination stops being linked to the checkout at all.
#
# Those generated files are gitignored, not committed: they used to be tracked,
# and then choosing any palette but this one left sixteen modified files behind
# and `ergon sync` refused to run. install.sh renders them before it links.
#
# (This paragraph named `bin/ergon-theme-render` for a long time. There has
# never been a file by that name.)

# -- neutrals: the ground the ramp sits on ----------------------------------
COOL_BG0=#1A1A2E          # window/bar background
COOL_BG1=#2A2A3E          # raised surface: code blocks, input fields
COOL_BG2=#3A3A4E          # hover, inactive border
COOL_FG0=#EDEDF8          # primary text
COOL_FG1=#D6D6FF          # secondary text (also ramp midpoint, t=0.5)
COOL_DIM=#A6A6BC          # comments, disabled
COOL_DIMMER=#9494B0       # rules, separators

# -- the ramp, strength 0.68 (t = 0, .25, .5, .75, 1) -----------------------
COOL_0=#ADFFFF
COOL_1=#C2EBFF
COOL_2=#D6D6FF
COOL_3=#EBC2FF
COOL_4=#FFADFF

# -- the same ramp at strength 0.25, for alerts only ------------------------
COOL_HOT_0=#40FFFF
COOL_HOT_4=#FF40FF

# -- off-ramp tints inherited from glow/cool.json ---------------------------
COOL_MINT=#D0FFEA         # strings, "good" states
COOL_BLUSH=#FFE0FF        # numbers, inline code

# -- semantic aliases: what the configs actually reference ------------------
# Change these to re-point a role without touching the ramp.
COOL_ACCENT=#ADFFFF       # normal accent
COOL_ACTIVE=#40FFFF       # focused window, active workspace
COOL_URGENT=#FF40FF       # critical battery, urgent notification
COOL_WARN=#EBC2FF
COOL_GOOD=#D0FFEA
COOL_INACTIVE=#3A3A4E
